+---
+id: task-002-monthly-order-export-csv
+type: task
+title: "Monthly order export CSV"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Monthly order export CSV
+
+## Request
+
+The accountant wants a monthly export of the orders, to load into her spreadsheet. The order book gets
+`exportMonth(month)`: given a month as `YYYY-MM`, it returns CSV text with a header line and one line per order placed
+in that month, by order number.
+
+- Columns, in this order: `order` (its number), `date` (as the order stores it), `customer` (the customer's name),
+  `total` (what the customer pays, in euros with two decimals, such as `12.50`). The export carries no email address
+  and no phone number.
+- A value containing a comma or a quote is quoted as CSV usually is.
+- A month with no orders gives the header line alone.
+- The first export is wanted at the end of October 2026.
+
+## Choices made here
+
+- A malformed month (not `YYYY-MM`) is refused with `invalid`, as the other order book operations refuse bad input.
+  `exportMonth` therefore returns a `Result<string>`, not a bare string.
+- `total` is the order's `totalCents` (items, less any discount, plus shipping), formatted with `formatEuros`.
+- A value is quoted when it contains a comma, a double quote, or a line break; a quote inside it is doubled.
+- Lines are joined with `\n`, with no trailing newline.
+- The month matches the order's stored date by its `YYYY-MM` prefix.
+- Contact details (email, phone) stay out of the export: the accountant's file carries the order number, date, customer
+  name and total only. This follows the rejection by Dana Okafor, recorded below.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- `exportMonth('2026-10')` gives the header and one line per October 2026 order, in order-number order.
+- Each line has four values: order number, date, customer name, total. No email address or phone number appears.
+- Each line's `total` is the order's `totalCents` in euros, two decimals.
+- A value with a comma or a quote is quoted, with its quotes doubled.
+- A month with no orders gives the header line alone.
+- A malformed month is refused with `invalid`.
+- The changelog records the change.
+
+## Rejection and rework
+
+Dana Okafor rejected the first version: the accountant's export must not carry customers' email addresses or phone
+numbers; the order number, date, customer name and total only. The rework removes the `email` and `phone` columns from
+`exportMonth`, its header and its tests, and the changelog entry.
+
+## Outcome
+
+- `OrderBook.exportMonth(month)` added in `src/domain/orders.ts`, returning `Result<string>`: a malformed month is refused
+  with `invalid`; otherwise the header and one line per order of the month, by number, with the total from `totalCents`
+  formatted by `formatEuros`. Values are quoted by `csvCell` when they hold a comma, a quote or a line break.
+- Tests added in `test/shop.test.ts` (`monthly export`): the red run failed on the missing method, then all 19 tests pass.
+- Changelog entry 1.6.0; `package.json` version bumped to 1.6.0 to match.
+- Rework after rejection: header is `order,date,customer,total`; 19 of 19 tests pass.
+- Not run: TypeScript type-check (`tsc` is not installed in this project).
